    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d95">

                <pb facs="0157" n="149" sameAs="frus:frus1938v01/pg149"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">852.00/7162: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Cuba</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Wright</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Habana</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-01-05T12:00:00-05:00">January 5,
                        1938—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 4:05 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2. Embassy’s despatch No. 374, December 20.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d95-f1"/> I have
                    just received a note dated December 30 from the Cuban Secretary of State in
                    which he refers to his <hi rendition="#italic">note verbale</hi> dated October
                    21 inviting the Government of the United States to join with the other American
                    countries in extending good offices to the warring factions in Spain.<anchor
                        n="3" xml:id="d95-f2"/> The Secretary of State informs me that 11 American
                    nations have supported his gesture; 6, while expressing hope for its success,
                    regret that they cannot associate themselves with it because of their policy of
                    nonintervention; and 2 condition their acceptance on a prior consultation with
                    the parties directly interested in the Spanish problem. After expressing thanks
                    for the welcome given the Cuban initiative and pointing out the special ties
                    between Spain and the American peoples of Spanish origin, the Secretary of State
                    announces that the Government of Cuba will consult the Government of the Spanish
                    Republic and General Francisco Franco confidentially concerning whether they
                    would be disposed to accept the tender of its good offices.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wright</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
